825	/**
826	 * Post-boot health verification for PROVISION.
827	 *
828	 * WHY: two real customer-facing incidents shipped because PROVISION reported
829	 * DONE on "no API call threw", never "the customer can actually reach what got
830	 * built" — a VM whose IP collided with unrelated infrastructure (network-layer,
831	 * nothing ever reachable) and, separately, EVERY game family's `docker run -d`
832	 * setup script creates no systemd unit, so a game can fail to start while the
833	 * container still shows "Up" (confirmed: Terraria hung on an interactive
834	 * world-selection prompt with no TTY, port never bound). Both would have been
835	 * caught by one real end-to-end check: actually try to reach the server the
836	 * way a customer would, before ever calling it ready.
837	 *
838	 * This module holds the pure retry/resolution logic (unit-testable, see
839	 * test/provision-health.test.ts) plus the real TCP I/O. worker/index.ts's
840	 * `provision()` calls `checkSshReachable` / `checkGamePortOpen` and — matching
841	 * this codebase's established failed-PROVISION convention — simply THROWS on
842	 * failure; the existing `runJob` catch path takes it from there (retry while
843	 * attempts remain, else ERROR + release the dedicated IP + notify the
844	 * customer). This module never touches Server/Job rows itself.
845	 */
846	import net from "node:net";
847	import dgram from "node:dgram";
