51	 * ADDITIVE to whichever plan the server is on, so a plan change must leave it
52	 * completely alone. Two things follow, and both are deliberate:
53	 *
54	 *  1. NOT DOUBLE-CHARGED. The proration below is a difference of PLAN monthly
55	 *     prices. The extra-disk surcharge is identical before and after the resize
56	 *     (same GB, same per-GB price), so it cancels out of the delta exactly — it
57	 *     must never be added here. It keeps being billed, unchanged, by the renewal
58	 *     sweep, which reads `extraDiskGB` directly.
59	 *  2. NOT LOST. Nothing in the resize path writes `extraDiskGB`, and the worker's
60	 *     RESIZE handler sizes the disk to `newPlan.diskGB + server.extraDiskGB` — so
61	 *     the customer keeps every GB they paid for. (Sizing to the bare plan disk
62	 *     would ask Proxmox to SHRINK the disk of any server holding extra storage,
63	 *     and the whole resize would hard-fail.)
64	 *
65	 * The disk-grow guard below intentionally compares PLAN disks, not effective
66	 * disks: since the extra is added to both sides, a target plan with an equal or
67	 * larger disk can never shrink the effective disk.
68	 */
69	export async function resizeQuote(serverId: string, planId: string): Promise<ResizeQuote> {
70	  const server = await loadServer(serverId);
71	  if (!server) throw new ResizeError("Not found");
72	  const newPlan = await loadPlan(planId);
73	  if (!newPlan?.active) throw new ResizeError("Plan not available.");
74	  if (newPlan.id === server.planId) throw new ResizeError("Server is already on this plan.");
75	  // A GAME server is not priced from `plan` at all, so it cannot be prorated
76	  // against it.
77	  //
78	  // Game tiers carry BOTH a base `planId` and a `gamePlanId`, and are sold at
79	  // `gamePlan.priceCents` — a FLAT, all-in price, typically LOWER than the base
80	  // plan they run on. The live row: `minecraft-mm8w85` sold at $4.99 on a Starter
81	  // base plan of $8.99. `renewalCharge` already treats a game plan as all-in with
82	  // no add-on lines; every proration path read `server.plan.priceCents` and was
83	  // blind to it.
84	  //
85	  // The dangerous direction is the CREDIT. lib/proration.ts rests on one
86	  // invariant — a prorated credit is strictly less than the cash actually
87	  // collected for those months — and that is the whole argument in
88	  // `resolveTermPromoPercent`. A tier sold at $9.99/mo flat on a $20.99 base plan
89	  // credits $20.99-worth per remaining month on a downgrade: real wallet money the
90	  // customer never paid, self-service.
91	  //
92	  // Today it is blocked only by the disk-monotonic guard below — in a catalogue
93	  // where price and disk both rise, no cheaper plan has equal-or-larger disk. That
94	  // is a coincidence of the current price list, not a protection; one cheap
95	  // high-disk plan opens it. lib/proration.ts's header calls out exactly this shape
96	  // about the pre-2026-07-25 bug, so it is refused explicitly rather than left
97	  // resting on an unrelated guard.
98	  if (server.gamePlanId) {
99	    throw new ResizeError(
100	      "Game server plans are fixed bundles — switch tiers from the game server's own settings, or contact support.",
101	    );
102	  }
103	  if (newPlan.diskGB < server.plan.diskGB) {
104	    throw new ResizeError("Disk storage can only grow — choose a plan with equal or larger disk.");
105	  }
106	  if (newPlan.priceCents < server.plan.priceCents) {
107	    throw new ResizeError("Downgrades aren't self-service yet — please contact support.");
108	  }
109	  if (!["RUNNING", "STOPPED"].includes(server.status)) throw new ResizeError("Server is busy.");
110	
111	  let prorateCents = 0;
112	  if (!server.isFree && server.expiresAt) {
113	    // Price the upgrade at the term discount AND the rate THIS SERVER'S TERM WAS
114	    // SOLD AT (`server.termPromoPercent`, persisted at every point a term is
115	    // bought). Using the raw list-price delta (as this did until 2026-07-25)
116	    // overcharged by the whole discount stack: on a 36-month term with a 30% promo
117	    // running, $1,044.45 to upgrade versus $665.08 to simply buy the same plan
118	    // outright for the same end date. Pricing off `currentPromo(now)` instead (as
119	    // it did until 2026-07-26) was worse: the customer picked the rate by picking
120	    // the date, upgrading under Cyber Monday's 45% and downgrading under the 15%
121	    // Launch Sale to pocket the spread. Shared with the downgrade credit and with
122	    // the client picker via lib/proration.ts so displayed, charged and credited
123	    // cannot drift apart.
124	    //
125	    // `remainingMonths` covers EVERY month left on `expiresAt` and takes no cap —
126	    // see the note on it. Charging the full stacked duration here is what makes the
127	    // mirror-image downgrade credit safe to pay out over the same duration: after
128	    // this charge the customer has genuinely paid the new plan's rate for every one
129	    // of those months. Capping this side alone would mint money on a round trip;
130	    // capping the credit side alone destroyed $362.88 of it until 2026-07-26.
131	    prorateCents = prorateUpgradeCents({
132	      fromMonthlyCents: server.plan.priceCents,
133	      toMonthlyCents: newPlan.priceCents,
134	      termMonths: server.termMonths,
135	      termPromoPercent: server.termPromoPercent,
136	      // The annual rate this server was SOLD at (frozen at order), falling back to the
137	      // LIVE setting only for legacy rows that predate the snapshot — so an admin retuning
138	      // `annual_discount_percent` between an upgrade and a later downgrade can't break the
139	      // round-trip-to-zero invariant (Finding 2). The downgrade credit reads the same value.
140	      annualDiscountPct: frozenAnnualDiscountPct(server) ?? (await annualDiscountPct()),
141	      monthsRemaining: remainingMonths(server.expiresAt, Date.now()),
142	    });
143	  }
144	  return { server, newPlan, prorateCents };
145	}
